      "latency_ms": 3555.1,
      "prompt_tokens": 11,
      "completion_tokens": 682,
      "output": "At its most fundamental level, **water** is a chemical compound and the most abundant substance on the Earth’s surface. It is essential for all known forms of life. \n\nHere is a breakdown of what water is, explained through different lenses:\n\n### 1. The Chemical View\n* **Chemical Formula:** $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}$\n* **Composition:** Each molecule of water is made up of **two hydrogen atoms** bonded to **one oxygen atom**.\n* **States of Matter:** Water is unique because it is the only natural substance on Earth that exists abundantly in three physical states:\n  * **Liquid** (water in rivers, oceans, and rain)\n  * **Solid** (ice, snow, and glaciers)\n  * **Gas** (water vapor, steam, and humidity)\n\n### 2. Physical and Chemical Properties\nWater has several unique and remarkable properties that make it vital for life:\n* **The \"Universal Solvent\":** Because of its chemical structure, water can dissolve more substances (like salt, sugar, and minerals) than any other liquid. This is how our bodies transport nutrients and how oceans maintain their chemical balance.\n* **Cohesion and Adhesion:** Water molecules like to stick to each other (cohesion—which creates surface tension, allowing some insects to walk on water) and to other surfaces (adhesion—which helps water move up the roots of plants).\n* **High Heat Capacity:** Water can absorb and release large amounts of heat with only small changes in its own temperature. This regulates the climate of the entire planet and helps living organisms maintain internal body temperature.\n* **Density Anomaly:** Unlike most substances, which become denser when they freeze, water actually *expands* and becomes less dense as ice. This is why ice floats, which prevents lakes and oceans from freezing solid from the bottom up, allowing aquatic life to survive the winter.\n\n### 3. The Ecological and Geological View\n* **Earth’s Coverage:** About **71% of the Earth's surface** is covered by water, mostly in oceans and seas (which hold about 96.5% of all Earth's water).\n* **Scarcity:** Despite how common it is, only about **2.5% of Earth's water is freshwater**, and most of that is locked away in glaciers, ice caps, or buried deep underground. Less than 1% of Earth's water is easily accessible for human use.\n* **The Water Cycle (Hydrologic Cycle):** Water is constantly recycled through evaporation (turning into vapor from heat), condensation (forming clouds), precipitation (rain and snow), and collection (flowing back into oceans and rivers). The water you drink today is the same water that dinosaurs drank millions of years ago!\n\n### 4. The Biological View\n* **The Basis of Life:** Water is the medium in which all the metabolic and cellular processes of life take place. \n* **Human Body:** The human body is made up of roughly **60% water**. It relies on water to regulate temperature, lubricate joints, flush out waste, and transport oxygen and nutrients through the blood.\n\nIn short, water is a simple molecule that acts as the ultimate life-support system for our planet."
